Lobium - navigator.gpu adapter identity.

WebGPU and WebGL are two independent descriptions of the same physical GPU, readable from one page in
two calls. Until this patch WebGL reported the persona's GPU while WebGPU reported the REAL one,
which is strictly worse than leaving either alone: a mismatch between them is a positive signal that
something is rewriting the first, whereas an honest pair is merely a host fingerprint.

GPUAdapterInfo fields are overridden from the config, which derives them from the same renderer
string the WebGL hooks report, so the two cannot name different hardware.

adapter_type_ is included in the override, and not only for completeness: CreateAdapterInfoForAdapter
derives isFallbackAdapter from it. A persona claiming a discrete GeForce while reporting a fallback
adapter contradicts itself inside a single object read, so the type has to move with the rest.

Chrome exposes vendor, architecture, subgroup sizes and isFallbackAdapter by default; device,
description and driver appear with WebGPU developer features enabled. All are set, because a
fingerprinter with those features enabled is exactly the caller worth being consistent for.

Hook point: third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc

diff --git a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc
--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.cc
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgpu/gpu_adapter.h"
 
+#include "components/lobium_fp/lobium_fp_config.h"
 #include "services/metrics/public/cpp/ukm_builders.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/bindings/core/v8/script_promise_resolver.h"
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/bindings/core/v8/v8_object_builder.h"
@@ -113,6 +114,47 @@ GPUAdapter::GPUAdapter(
   subgroup_max_size_ = info.subgroupMaxSize;
   power_preference_ = powerProperties.powerPreference;
 
+  // Lobium: report the persona's GPU through navigator.gpu, the same one WebGL reports.
+  //
+  // WebGPU is a SECOND, independent hardware identity. Left alone it names the real device while
+  // WebGL names the persona's, and a page reads both in two calls - so the pair is a contradiction,
+  // which is worse than either surface being honest on its own. Overriding here, right after Dawn's
+  // values are unpacked, covers both shapes of adapter.info: the default reduced set (vendor,
+  // architecture, subgroup sizes, isFallbackAdapter) and the fuller set exposed when WebGPU
+  // developer features are enabled (device, description, driver).
+  //
+  // adapter_type_ is included deliberately, because CreateAdapterInfoForAdapter() below derives
+  // isFallbackAdapter from it - a CPU adapter reports true. Any software backend is a CPU adapter,
+  // so a persona claiming real silicon while advertising the fallback bit would contradict its own
+  // renderer string. Empty fields keep Dawn's real values, so a partial config degrades to honesty
+  // rather than to an incoherent mixture.
+  if (const lobium::LobiumFpConfig* cfg = lobium::LobiumFpConfig::Current();
+      cfg && cfg->webgpu.present) {
+    const lobium::WebGpuConfig& gpu_cfg = cfg->webgpu;
+    if (!gpu_cfg.vendor.empty()) {
+      vendor_ = String::FromUtf8(gpu_cfg.vendor);
+    }
+    if (!gpu_cfg.architecture.empty()) {
+      architecture_ = String::FromUtf8(gpu_cfg.architecture);
+    }
+    if (!gpu_cfg.device.empty()) {
+      device_ = String::FromUtf8(gpu_cfg.device);
+    }
+    if (!gpu_cfg.description.empty()) {
+      description_ = String::FromUtf8(gpu_cfg.description);
+    }
+    if (!gpu_cfg.driver.empty()) {
+      driver_ = String::FromUtf8(gpu_cfg.driver);
+    }
+    if (gpu_cfg.adapter_type == "discrete") {
+      adapter_type_ = wgpu::AdapterType::DiscreteGPU;
+    } else if (gpu_cfg.adapter_type == "integrated") {
+      adapter_type_ = wgpu::AdapterType::IntegratedGPU;
+    } else if (gpu_cfg.adapter_type == "cpu") {
+      adapter_type_ = wgpu::AdapterType::CPU;
+    }
+  }
+
   features_ = MakeFeatureNameSet(GetHandle());
 
   GPUSupportedLimits::ComboLimits limits;
